Z||000000||The Beauty||03-26-2001||08:44 PM||||Hi everybody I'm new here. I recently went to the Daytona Fiero Show with my family and WOW!!! what an event.  In the process I began to understand the enigma of the fiero. Now I simply have to have one.  My Dad and Boyfriend already have their own. (My Dad had one entered in '85 GT Stock he got 2nd in his class <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).  Anyway I'm trying to see if any of you have any advice on where to find a fiero. I'm looking for an '84, '85, or formula '88 (with the pointy front), a solid frame, good body, and sturdy engine.  Ultimately I'd like to add intakes on the sides behind to doors, add a kit to the front bumper, add leather to the interior, add power to the engine and give it a nice paint job.  Once again this is an "Ultimately."  Any advice or help you can give me on starting would be really appreciated.<BR>     <BR>     Sincerely,<BR>     The Beauty<BR>     Inspired South Florida Fiero Enthusiast<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000001||87GT||03-26-2001||08:51 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!  I was at the show too as well as quite a few of the PFF members.  I see your from Florida too.  You didnt by chance come up to the Suncoast Fiero tent did you?  I remember a family coming up to me at the tent and I spoke to them for a little while.  I know they were from South Florida some where.  I hope you enjoyed the show as much as we all did.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>As far as looking for a fiero, you will find a 84 or 85 for a much cheaper price than you would a 88 Formula. But, the 88 Formula does have the revised suspension and braking and a V6.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||DavidHenshaw||03-26-2001||09:01 PM||||Welcome Beauty. Unlike you and 87 GT I did not make it to Daytona, so Boo Hoo for me. Maybe next year. I am planning on going to Carlisle in May however.<P>I have heard some on the Forum say the 88's are more expensive to buy parts for and the parts are harder to find. IMHO I would stay with the 85 thru 87 model years. My only other suggestion is to buy a V6. All those extra horses are there for a reason.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cheever2||03-26-2001||09:52 PM||||Here is where I'd be looking if I were you...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.autotrader.com/findacar/findacar_form2.jtmpl?LNX=LIBSCATHOTOOLBRK&address=77459&make=PONT&Search.x=12&Search.y=9" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autotrader.com/findacar/findacar_form2.jtmpl?LNX=LIBSCATHOTOOLBRK&address=77459&make=PONT&Search.x=12&Search.y=9</A> <P>Good luck, and welcome!<BR>With a username like "Beauty", you KNOW we're gonna want to see some pics!  C'mon now, don't be shy...<P><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000004||TwisT||03-26-2001||11:11 PM||||Welcome to the Fiero world!<P>You'll find some good deals in the mall,  as well as other places on the 'Net.  Lot's of Fiero's on Ebay all the time...<P>The more common (and usually best priced) Fieros are the '84's to '87's.  I prefer GT's,  but if you're in this forum long enough,  you'll figure out your favorite.  Just a matter of taste...<P>Good luck,  and once again,  welcome...<P>Brett<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/italbanner3.JPG"> <P>...Website coming soon!||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||03-27-2001||12:50 AM||||Cheever is right about the autotrader.  But try this link, it is right here in SFL.  BTW you just missed a nice 88GT with 30K mi on it from central FL.  It was schweet!!! After I bought my 2nd one from the original owner (and found on the AT), I went to go get the '88 and it was gone.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
